# Project

mararx.com is Denis's personal photo journal and travel diary.

The website is primarily for publishing:
- photography;
- short observations;
- longer travel stories;
- locations and routes from trips, mainly around Georgia.

This is not:
- a commercial photographer portfolio;
- a SaaS product;
- a social network;
- a complex publishing platform.

# Main sections

## Journal

Journal is the home page.

It should include:
- a chronological feed of recent photos;
- a year filter at the top;
- large year labels with one active year;
- trip or story name associated with each photo;
- large photo preview on click;
- optional caption;
- optional link to the related Story.

## Stories

Stories contains cards for individual trips or photo stories.

A Story page may include:
- photos;
- optional text blocks;
- title and date;
- location;
- coordinates;
- embedded map or route.

Use the name "Stories", not "Journeys".

## About

About explains:
- who the author is;
- the idea behind the project;
- the approach to travel and photography.

# Design direction

The site should feel:
- visual;
- atmospheric;
- restrained;
- editorial;
- contemporary;
- personal.

Photography must remain the dominant visual element.

Avoid:
- dashboard layouts;
- excessive cards;
- generic SaaS styling;
- excessive rounded containers;
- heavy animation;
- decorative UI that competes with photos;
- unnecessary text;
- template-like portfolio design.

# Development approach

- Build directly in code.
- Do not create a separate prototype first.
- Keep the architecture simple.
- Use reusable components only where they reduce duplication.
- Do not add a CMS, authentication, admin panel, or cloud storage integration unless requested.
- Use placeholder content when real content is unavailable.
- Iterate section by section from browser screenshots.
- Prefer the simplest implementation that supports Journal, Stories, About, and mobile usability.
